# Reply to Adjournment Motion: Fires in Madras City
Tamil title: ஒத்திவைப்புத் தீர்மானம் - பதிலுரை: சென்னை நகரில் தீ விபத்துகள்

Debate held on 6 July 1967.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Mr. Speaker, what I submitted said only that such talk was strongly circulating among the public. They have left the grammar aside and not attended to the joining of the words. I wish to make clear to the members that I did not add my own views to it.

At first the police thought these fires might have occurred accidentally. Since yesterday, however, an opinion has grown among the police too that this may be a deliberately planned conspiracy. That view has spread strongly among them, and they have undertaken the necessary inquiries and measures.

What I recorded was only what the public in those localities were saying when I toured them. The hon. Leader of the Opposition said, “If this has happened, and if our party is involved, we are ready for any judgment.” There will be no favour or indulgence toward anyone in this matter. Whoever they may be, those involved will be severely punished.

Mr. Speaker, the concern, interest and urgency shown by every member of this House regarding this question are truly welcome. Though many fires of this kind have occurred, this one is especially distressing.

Yesterday, while I was inspecting the site of one fire, smoke suddenly billowed a quarter of a furlong away and people ran screaming. Immediately afterwards, people rushed in alarm toward another side where another fire was said to have started.

People outside say that, since there would not even have been time to set each fire, explosive substances such as phosphorus may have been scattered beforehand so that they would ignite when the appropriate time came. Earlier fires normally occurred because flame spread from a cooking hearth or lamp. From the manner, scale and pattern of these fires, members of this House will agree that they were not accidental.

There is no need, however, for arguments over who did it. If anyone committed such acts, nobody would hesitate to condemn them as enemies of society. Whatever party such antisocial persons may claim to belong to, I firmly believe that a responsible leader of that party would not truly recognise such a person as belonging to it.

Until now the police thought the events accidental. They have changed that view and now believe they may have been planned.

Our Leader of the Opposition said that all the punctuation in the *Dina Thanthi* report was correct. The report says: “There is also talk that political enemies are taking revenge upon hut-dwellers who support the Kazhagam.” When a tragic incident occurs, many different thoughts and rumours naturally arise.

The suffering people said such things to me. Mothers told me, “Look at this injustice, son! This is our condition because we voted for you.” They expressed their anguish in different words. I have already explained that the pattern of these fires was such that everyone might suspect conspiracy.

It is therefore a matter the police must actively uncover. Rather than politicians continuing to express opinions, the police have assumed responsibility for conducting an intensive investigation: is such a conspiracy under way, and if so, who is responsible? I firmly believe they will succeed.

**Thiru P. G. Karuthiruman:** Forgive the interruption, Mr. Speaker. Only now does the Chief Minister cite a newspaper report as evidence. Earlier, he rejected the newspaper reports we cited and said they need not be accepted. Let that remain aside. All I ask is whether the Chief Minister himself believes there is “political enmity.” What is his view of the Congress?

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** “Congress” is a general word. Every party may have several levels and people of many different dispositions. They will not expect me to speak about this at greater length.

About ten days ago, we received information that a Congress speaker had said, “Not even months—within days, let us see what becomes of them.” There is nothing improper in the public wondering whether that speech is connected with these events. [Commotion.]

I do not regard that speech as Congress speaking. In my mind, “Congress speech” means the speech of people of the standing of our friend Thiru Karuthiruman, Thiru Kamaraj and Thiru Bhaktavatsalam. I have long regarded other antisocial speech merely as the speech of people who make their living by using the Congress. [Commotion.]

In referring to it, I did not say “Congress is doing this” or “Congressmen are doing this.” The statement I submitted contains no such idea. I said only that the public were talking in that manner.

The police suspect that some conspiracy has taken place and are investigating it. We must await the outcome of their inquiry. As Thiru Karuthiruman said, whoever is responsible will face action of some kind, whether prosecution and punishment in court or condemnation by the public.

Our first task now is to comfort the affected people. Rather than debate who instigated it or which party stands behind it, we must relieve those people, remove the fear in their minds, give them protection, and provide proper housing. We must consider what should be done through an appropriate long-term plan and devote our time to that.

If instead we adjourn the business of the House and debate this, each person will begin speaking as Thiru Karuthiruman and I are now doing. The debate will spread precisely the opinion we believe should not spread.

Moreover, while the police suspect conspiracy and are investigating, adjourning the House to discuss it may obstruct their work. I therefore ask every member to use his ability and time to dispel public fear and comfort those who have suffered. I submit to the Speaker that suspending the House's business for this debate would neither be proper nor necessary and would yield no useful result.

**Thiru P. G. Karuthiruman:** Mr. Speaker, the Chief Minister's reply is not entirely satisfactory. On one side he says everyone must cooperate; on the other he says this is what the public are saying. Even if a judicial inquiry is held, we accept it and are ready for it. We say it should be done.

In the present situation, if allegations and defamatory complaints are made against any political party and everything is labelled “politics,” I seriously doubt whether democracy or merely party rule can function properly. It is not good to say anything merely because a party has influence.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I tell the Leader of the Opposition that I did not say a party sat down, passed a resolution and carried this out. It may be the act of people connected with a party at the lowest level, people without understanding who miscalculate that they can gain greatly from such deeds. I do not even say that it is so. Would the All India Congress Committee pass a party resolution for an act like this?

**Thiru P. G. Karuthiruman:** Mr. Speaker, my humble request is that the position must be firm and not ambiguous. If each side raises suspicion and defamation against the other, democracy cannot function. If another party begins following the course they describe, it will travel down a very wrong path.

On an earlier occasion when a disaster occurred, a judicial inquiry was arranged. If clarity is required concerning their statement that there was a political motive against our party, and if democracy is to endure, an inquiry must be arranged. If the public are to form a sound opinion, there must be a judicial inquiry. Through you, I wish to learn whether the Chief Minister accepts arranging one.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** That is precisely what I said at the outset. The police are now working to obtain complete information. After their conclusion is known, if a judicial inquiry is necessary, I have no hesitation in appointing one. A judicial inquiry may be held. But the police must first investigate and discover the facts. That responsibility has been entrusted to them.

The question of the party was raised. I repeat: nobody anywhere imagined or said that a party would plan and carry out acts of this kind. But in every party there may be some people who believe such actions can bring advantage to their party.

The hon. Thiru Karuthiruman himself knows the nature of the conduct, speech and activities of certain people in the party to which he belongs. I understand that he himself has been affected by them. He may choose to disregard that. But he knows such people exist in a party. No party can completely prevent every such person from acting.

[Several lines at the end of Image 7 are damaged.]

The public themselves would not believe that responsible, upright and reasonable people at the proper level of any party had committed this act…

**Thiru P. G. Karuthiruman:** Mr. Speaker, I greatly expected the Chief Minister to speak clearly. But his answer will again encourage unjust and defamatory talk against our party. My humble question is simply whether he now agrees to refer the matter to a judicial inquiry.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I have stated it categorically. The police are engaged in investigating the matter. Once that stage is complete, the question of a judicial inquiry will be considered.

---NOTES---
The eight scans preserve the debate's conclusion. A short passage at the end of Image 7 is damaged and is marked rather than reconstructed. The headline's opening OCR is damaged, but the date and subject are clear. The translation carefully distinguishes public rumour, police suspicion, and a formal Government allegation; Anna explicitly denies accusing Congress as an organisation. “Phosphorus” follows the source's பாஸ்பரம் and remains an allegation circulating among bystanders, not an established forensic finding. The Tamil source remains verbatim.
